 "notes": [
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Some shops use black soybeans (kuromame), soaked and simmered with salt the same way, for about an hour.</li><li>Plain yellow soybeans aren't used for daifuku.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Black soybeans instead"
  },
  {
   "html": "<p>Ready-cooked salted akaendo (sold for mitsumame) skip steps 1–2. Pat them dry.</p>",
   "title_html": "Cooked peas"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Shiratamako gives the softest, stretchiest mochi.</li><li>Mochiko (sweet rice flour) works too: use <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"mass\" data-u=\"g\" data-a=\"100\" data-s=\"0\">100 g</span> with about <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"ml\" data-a=\"110\" data-s=\"0\">110 ml</span> water, and expect it a little firmer.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Shiratamako or mochiko"
  },
  {
   "html": "<p>Steam the batter in a heatproof bowl over boiling water for 15–20 minutes, stirring every 5, until translucent.</p>",
   "title_html": "No microwave"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Best within a few hours.</li><li>Covered at cool room temperature they last until evening.</li><li>In the fridge the mochi hardens within a day.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Keeping"
  },
  {
   "html": "<p>At 1000 W, cut each burst by about a third and check sooner.</p>",
   "title_html": "Microwave power"
  }
 ],
